1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:15,750
When Music Tribe open-sourced the firmware for the Behringer KICK, BE 0700-AAM, it came with one critical catch: the software was tightly coupled to physical hardware, relying on proprietary modules and custom serial drivers.

2
00:00:15,700 --> 00:00:21,637
Without the physical groovebox on your desk, developing or testing was virtually impossible.

3
00:00:21,637 --> 00:00:31,887
Enter 0700-AAM-SIM: a zero-dependency desktop simulator and virtual hardware harness built in modern C++17.

4
00:00:32,544 --> 00:00:38,556
At the heart of the system is a wire-accurate implementation of Music Tribe's binary ComProtocol.

5
00:00:38,506 --> 00:00:45,794
Every single packet header, command ID, and CRC-8 checksum is deterministically emulated.

6
00:00:45,794 --> 00:00:59,094
Using our headless scenario runner, complex musical performances can be replayed and verified against golden state traces in CI pipelines, with four out of four unit test suites passing in less than two tenths of a second.

7
00:00:59,756 --> 00:01:06,043
0700-AAM-SIM models the complete physical control surface.

8
00:01:05,993 --> 00:01:17,656
Sixteen velocity-sensitive RGB pads across eight banks, four assignable Q-Link continuous encoders, the central jog wheel, capacitive touch strip, and transport controls.

9
00:01:17,656 --> 00:01:29,193
It even includes synthetic gesture injection for the AI camera, CV gate output logging, and virtual pseudo-terminals, allowing the real firmware application to connect unmodified.

10
00:01:29,848 --> 00:01:39,535
Licensed under AGPL version three or later, 0700-AAM-SIM brings true independence to the embedded audio community.

11
00:01:39,485 --> 00:01:49,510
Whether you are hacking the firmware, creating custom sequencing algorithms, or running automated test suites, you can now build and iterate entirely in software.

12
00:01:49,510 --> 00:01:54,510
Clean code, deterministic tests, and zero hardware barriers.
